@startuml

title The schedule of a report definition

hide empty description

[*] --> unscheduled : the definition is created\n(fsm state active)

unscheduled --> scheduled : schedule\n(create the grant, put the job,\nrecord both ids)
scheduled --> unscheduled : unschedule\n(delete the job, revoke the grant)
scheduled --> scheduled : fire\n(a run under the grant)
unscheduled --> unscheduled : manual trigger\n(a one-run grant)
scheduled --> suspended : a step reports grant_lapsed,\nor reconciliation finds\na job and no active grant\n(fsm state suspended)
suspended --> scheduled : schedule again\n(a new consent, a new grant)
suspended --> unscheduled : unschedule
unscheduled --> [*] : delete
scheduled --> [*] : delete\n(delete the job, revoke the grant)
suspended --> [*] : delete\n(delete the job)

note bottom of suspended
  The job stays, and its firings are refused
  by the trigger, so the scheduler records them
  as failed with the reason.
end note

@enduml
